WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
To build a native level application, the standard is strict.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:10.880
When data changes, the user interface must react instantly with zero perceptible latency.

3
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:19.320
React native 0.76 and beyond achieves this by moving to a strictly bridgeless JSI first architecture.

4
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.640
Previously, JavaScript and native threads communicated over an asynchronous bridge.

5
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.400
Every UI update had to be serialized, queued, and deserialized,

6
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.560
which caused visual lack.

7
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.200
For engineers adopting the new architecture,

8
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.320
the challenge is understanding exactly how the isolated components of the system,

9
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.960
Hermes, Cogen, and Fabric wire together end-to-end.

10
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.000
We are going to trace the full execution pipeline,

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.000
starting from pre-compiled bytecode on the device,

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.880
all the way to a synchronous UI commit.

13
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.640
Understanding this pipeline allows engineers to produce the deterministic performance

14
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.280
required for complex applications.

15
00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:07.160
To understand the pipeline, we have to look at what occurs before a single react component even attempts to render.

16
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:13.160
This timeline contrasts the old, just-in-time model with Hermes ahead of time compilation.

17
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.040
The device never parses raw JavaScript.

18
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:22.000
Compilation happens at build time, delivering only the pre-compiled.hbc binary.

19
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.600
Instead of generating runtime machine code,

20
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.360
Hermes interprets a register-based instruction set directly.

21
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.080
The only exception is during debug builds.

22
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.000
Here, Hermes eases jit-free interpretation of text JavaScript

23
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.440
so that you maintain accurate, line-by-line source maps.

24
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.520
Shifting compilation to build time removes parsing and execution overhead

25
00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.920
from the application's cold start phase.

26
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:49.480
In the past, the standard method for improving startup times was using RAM bundles

27
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.000
to load only the specific modules required into memory.

28
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.680
If you are using Hermes, you must stop using RAM bundles.

29
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.160
The .hbc format inherently surpasses it in efficiency on its own.

30
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:06.280
The modern optimization for managing module loading is lazy module evaluation

31
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.640
specifically through the in-line requires configuration.

32
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.200
This flow chart compares execution paths.

33
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.960
A top-level require evaluates the module when the file loads.

34
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.000
The in-line configuration defers evaluation until a specific function needs the byte code.

35
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.080
For apps with large dependencies, this lowers the time to interactive.

36
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.200
Profiling in Android Studios Perfetto, you track these gains by monitoring the load bundle method

37
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.640
in Hermes executor and call function within runtime.

38
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.960
In the new architecture, performance gains come from strictly deferring byte code evaluation

39
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.520
until it is required by the execution path.

40
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:45.960
Once Hermes byte code is evaluated, it needs a way to safely communicate with native device APIs.

41
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.120
The legacy architecture relied on magic strings and runtime dictionary lookups.

42
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.040
If a method name was misspelled, it would often fail silently during execution.

43
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.720
Code Gen solves this problem.

44
00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.680
It reads a TypeScript specification file and acts as a build-time generator,

45
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.720
outputting a native C++ header file.

46
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.840
Your workflow simplifies to taking this generated header

47
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.960
and subclassing it to build out your native module.

48
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.920
Because the native implementation is forced to conform to the structure of the generated header,

49
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.120
you have Type Safety across the JavaScript to native boundary.

50
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.240
Code Gen shifts the burden of type validation from the user's device at runtime

51
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.480
to the developer's machine at build-time,

52
00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.800
eliminating a category of native crashes.

53
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.640
Knowing how the native code is typed leads directly into how that code is loaded into memory

54
00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:34.920
during application startup.

55
00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.760
The old architecture penalized boot times because every native module was eagerly instantiated

56
00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.320
when the app launched, regardless of user behavior.

57
00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.680
This memory map illustrates the turbo-module paradigm of lazy instantiation.

58
00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.240
Core startup nodes initialize immediately,

59
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.240
but specialized modules like this Bluetooth component remain dormant at boot.

60
00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.920
The module is exclusively created at the exact millisecond JavaScript fires a call to

61
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.600
turbo-moduleregistry.get.

62
00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:07.640
Instead of wasting memory, it acts exactly like a dormant native hardware bridge,

63
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.280
instantly waking up and locking into gear on demand.

64
00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.640
Large-scale applications lower their memory footprint because unvisited screens and unused

65
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:20.760
features never trigger initialization cycles. The ultimate test of this architecture is whether

66
00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.240
it can execute a native function, return a value to JavaScript, and update the UI

67
00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:30.360
all within the exact same frame. This timeline represents a critical rule of the system.

68
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.680
There is zero event loop tick between a turbo-module call and its synchronous return,

69
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.640
bounded here by a 16.6 millisecond V-sync frame.

70
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:42.760
Step one begins when JavaScript calls a function like set auth token, prompting React to schedule

71
00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:49.480
a discrete priority update. Step two, reacts reconciler processes that data and produces a new

72
00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:57.400
element tree. Step three, through the JavaScript interface, or JSI, the fabric C++ layer receives

73
00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:04.040
this new tree synchronously. Step four, the mathematical layout for the UI is computed directly

74
00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:10.040
within the C++ layer. Finally, a mounting transaction is dispatched to the UI thread,

75
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:15.800
updating the native views just before the frame reaches its limit. The legacy A synchronous Q would

76
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.400
have required a round trip delay, missing this frame and forcing a visual stutter. Now, execution

77
00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.920
remains locked to the display's refresh rate. By interlocking ahead of time compiled bytecode,

78
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:33.160
generated C++ bindings and synchronous JSI, React Native achieves deterministic performance.

